A||||28||frontal lobe||If you could afford it...||||1||
Z||000000||frontal lobe||08-15-2001||03:11 PM||||In another thread, another forum member mentioned that nobody that owns a Fiero is rich enough to be in a certain club.  Details not relevant.  This is NOT an attack against that member.<P>A different forum member answered back that although she could afford a different car, she decided to get a Fiero and spend the difference on other things (or save it).<P>So that made me curious.  Who would trade in their Fiero and add some cash if they had it to spend, and would be owning and driving a different car right now if they could?  And if you would, what car would it be?<P>In other words, who is driving a Fiero because they really want to, versus that is all they could afford?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Bluebottlejunky||08-15-2001||03:21 PM||||bah, id keep the fiero..but still buy a nice car unless the Fiero breaks.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/smiley_damon.gif[/IMG]Mike[IMG]http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/smiley_damon.gif"> <BR>85'GT 4sp<BR>AIM: <B>KilleRedGT</B><p>[This message has been edited by Bluebottlejunky (edited 08-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||EdsB52||08-15-2001||03:25 PM||||I think what ray meant was that the predominant Fiero owner is of the lower/lower-middle class variety. I'm talking economic class. He's right, we are. Are there millionaires out there that own Fieros? Of course. But the average Fiero owner probably makes an average of $25k per year.||||reg||1
Z||000003||frontal lobe||08-15-2001||03:45 PM||||I agree, EdsB52.  The millionaires out there with Fieros probably have other sports cars  as well.<P>I was not picking on rayb, just thought it was interesting.<P>In my case, I could afford to buy more cars, but wouldn't have anywhere to put them (one minivan, one family sedan take up 2 spaces in the garage).  <P>So I was just curious, if you could only afford ONE for-fun car (and had the money for more expensive) or only had room for ONE, would it still be a Fiero?||||reg||1
Z||000004||84Bill||08-15-2001||03:47 PM||||I'd prolly take up Fiero collecting as a hobby, Since I like to work on them I'd sink alot of money into resto work. I'll probably never sell my Fieros.||||reg||14
Z||000005||ChadMan||08-15-2001||03:48 PM||||What a great question.  I'm sure we'll get some interesting responses.<P>When I bought my first Fiero, I was a college student making $8000/yr.  It was the nicest car I could afford.<P>Now that I'm several years out of school and doing much better financially, it's still the nicest car I can afford, because I'd have to spend at least $10000 to get anything significantly nicer.<P>Think about it...<BR>* Affordable Porsches (944 and 928) are expensive to repair.<BR>* I don't fit in Miatas.<BR>* Pre-'94 Mustangs are lots of fun, but they rust and their brakes suck.<BR>* I can't see out of the '93- F-body, and the pre-'93's are all ragged out.<BR>* I hate the way FWD cars handle, which rules out Saturns and rice. (Don't flame me.  It's just my opinion.  I can't help it if I'm right.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> )<BR>* The 80's RX-7 and 300ZX were cool, but I'm not in love with their looks.  And are they REALLY any nicer than a Fiero?<P>The only sub-$10K car I'd gladly trade my Fiero for is a nicer Fiero.<P>There are plenty of nice cars in the $10-$15K range.  4.6 Mustangs.  C4 Corvettes. Last-gen RX-7's, 300ZX's and Supras.  944S2 and 944 Turbo.  Lots and lots of BMW's.  But I need a $300/mo car payment like I need a bag on my hip.<P>When/if I CAN afford a nicer car, I want to keep my Fiero too, for old times' sake.||||reg||4
Z||000006||JSocha||08-15-2001||04:06 PM||||I'll keep collecting Fieros to put next to my 1987 Fiero GT; 1987 Chrysler LeBaron; 1989 Chrysler New Yorker Landau; 1997 Dodge 1500; 2001 Dodge 3500 and the 197x-8x Lamborghini Countach S I am going to have (not the kit as I once was going to do) once I find a way to get past any ND restrictions for these vehicles that may exsist.<P>Thinking on a Porche as well.  Not sure though.||||reg||1
Z||000007||RiceCooker||08-15-2001||04:14 PM||||Excellent topic<P>if i won say, more than $500,000 for example i would go out and buy my dream car Ferrari 355 GTS. <BR>Other than that, I would drive my second ultimate dream car,a V8 fiero. Very custom in every way somewhat close to the blue  chop top. I truly love fieros and don't just drive them because they are (relatively) inexpensive.||||reg||6
Z||000008||MinnGreenGT||08-15-2001||04:22 PM||||Hmmm.... as for the class thing, yes and no. I Agree that most Fiero owners are not righ (although there are some exceptions).  Even if I had a ton more money, the Fiero would still be my choice of car.  I'd just have the 3800 s/c w/N20 and a bunch of other decked-out stuff.  I've only considered a few other cars: a last-model Toyota Supra TwinTurbo would be nice.  But still... I couldn't give up my Fiero.  When I asked my wife if I could by TerryK's 3800 s/c car, she pointed out that the only way I could afford it without taking out a loan (which was not acceptable to her) was to sell my current '87 GT.  mmmmm-tough choice.  I decided to hang on to my money-pit GT and just keep adding to it!  I hope to NEVER sell my Fiero!<P>And if I had REALLY too much money- I'd get a Ferrari- probably a F355GTB, but still keep working on the Fiero!<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/grngt.jpg">   <P>Medium Metallic Green 87 GT<BR>3.1L High Output<BR>4 Spd OD Tranny<BR>Open (read: Loud) Exhaust<BR>and More!<BR>-Rob Bartlett<p>[This message has been edited by MinnGreenGT (edited 08-15-2001).]||||reg||1
